{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n45 \n \n \nII.  Methods/Research Operations \n  \nB. Discussion of Method/Protocol \nFlame Design and other ACME experiments were configured, but not conducted, by the \nISS crew.  The crew\u2019s role was to set up the hardware, gas bottles, etc. for each set of \ntests.  The most common changes included the gas bottles on the Combustion Integrated \nRack (CIR) and the mass flow controllers on the modular ACME chamber insert. \n \nAll ACME testing was commanded from the ground, specifically from the Glenn ISS \nPayload Operations Center (GIPOC).  The science portion of ACME testing was conducted \nin a nominally automated mode using pre-programmed scripts.  While it was possible to \nmake some \u2018manual\u2019 changes outside of the script, e.g., to approach a flame\u2019s soot limit, \nduring a test that was not done in any ACME testing, neglecting minor exceptions, e.g., to \nterminate tests after the flames had extinguished. \n \nTesting was not conducted when thrusters fired or spacecraft docked or undocked from \nISS to ensure an acceptable acceleration environment. \n \nData was downlinked between each set of tests, i.e., day of testing, to allow for \npreliminary analysis and requisite planning for subsequent testing. \n \n \nIII.  Results \nG. Baselined Activities Completed \u2013 Pre-flight, In-flight, Post-Flight \nA total of 326 flames were ignited for the Flame Design experiment during Increments \n59/50 and 66, where 188 and 138 flames were ignited in the two increments, respectively. \n \nThe first-round tests featured normal flames, where gaseous fuel was ejected from a \nporous spherical burner into an oxygen/nitrogen atmosphere.  In contrast, the second \nround - except for its first test day - was carried out with inverse flames, in which the \noxidizer is ejected from the burner into a fuel/nitrogen atmosphere.  As a result, the \nsecond-round flames were \u2018inside out.\u2019  The \u2018interruption\u2019 in the Flame Design testing was \nintentionally planned years in advance to enable the PI team to review the initial results \nin detail and with that knowledge plan the second round of tests to maximize its scientific \noutcome. \n \nFlame Design did not have living subjects like a life science experiment, where the tests \nwere conducted with the gaseous fuel ethylene (C2H4).  It is a simple hydrocarbon gas for \nwhich the chemistry is well understood and it is typically highly sooting because of its", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 45}}